            <ArticleTitle>Symptoms other than pain in palliative care in Portugal.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>335-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1049909114520715</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study the burden of patients' symptoms other than pain and their treatment by Portuguese palliative care teams.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Of the 21 Portuguese palliative care teams identified, 10 accepted to participate. Data from all patients observed on the 18th week of 2011 were collected.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">One hundred and sixty-four patients were included in this study. One hundred and fifty-one patients (92%) had cancer. The patients' median age was 71 years (16-95) and 84 (51%) were females. The main symptoms were fatigue (116 [85%]) and depression/sadness (107 [65%]). Many different drugs were used for symptom control and other aims. Symptoms and drugs used here are similar to those practiced in other countries.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The practice of palliative care in Portugal seems to be similar to those in other countries.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© The Author(s) 2014.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
